{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VKL5NnGzyqNVnA5xsjJsXk","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AMERICAN FOOTBALL FIASCO\n2\t\n3\t**The build plan.** Source: American football (over-the-top arcade style). Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the\n4\thandoff package). Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1. Planned on 2026-11-03 by claude-opus-5-5 (reasoning effort max)\n5\tfrom `TASK.md` and `STAGE.md`. This plan is for the developers who will build the game from `TASK.md` and this file\n6\talone, possibly other AI models working without the planner. Every engine and hub name it uses was checked against\n7\tthe real files during planning (engine commit `4390a9852fe0`, hub commit `f8835e4824fb`).\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 0. Read this first\n12\t\n13\t### 0.1 The title\n14\tPicked the way TASK.md asks. 50 playful riffs on \"American Football\" were brainstormed silently and one was drawn\n15\twith a real random number generator: Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`, which uses OS entropy, gave index 33, which is\n16\t**American Football Fiasco**. It shows on the title screen as `AMERICAN FOOTBALL FIASCO`. Do not re-roll it.\n17\t\n18\t### 0.2 Names and deliverables\n19\t- GameTitle, file-safe: `AmericanFootballFiasco`. SOURCE, file-safe: `AmericanFootball`.\n20\t- The dev folder (all dev files go here, inside this folder):\n21\t  `AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831/`\n22\t- The deliverable: `AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831.html` in this\n23\t  folder's root, beside TASK.md. It is one self-contained HTML file and runs from `file://`.\n24\t- `exit-interview.json` beside it, in exactly the shape TASK.md gives. Check that it parses.\n25\t- If your TASK.md gives another model name or timestamp in those brackets, use your names and keep the GameTitle and\n26\t  SOURCE parts.\n27\t\n28\t### 0.3 Working rules (from TASK.md, repeated here so none is missed)\n29\t- Read only the files you need. Other agents share this directory, this machine's ports and its processes.\n30\t- Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), run\n31\t  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port it prints, and check that the page it serves is your game\n32\t  before you trust a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID. Never use pkill, killall, `fuser -k`, or\n33\t  kill whatever holds a port.\n34\t- `/tmp` on this machine is a small tmpfs that other agents fill up (it was full during planning). Put clones, caches\n35\t  and test output in your dev folder (`.cache/`, `shots/`, `check-output/`), not in `/tmp`.\n36\t- Tests: one command that prints only failures and a one-line count (§21). Rerun it only after you change code it\n37\t  covers. Take screenshots only for what a script can't check. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review,\n38\t  judge or audit.\n39\t- Done means no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features. Every feature in this plan ships, or this plan says how it\n40\t  is cut.\n41\t- The Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback go in the in-game About/Credits screen\n42\t  and in your final chat reply. The same answers go in `exit-interview.json` (§23). Write \"unknown\" for anything you\n43\t  can't confirm. The Plan field is `PLAN.md`.\n44\t\n45\t### 0.4 How to read this plan\n46\t- §1–2: what the game is, and where each TASK.md requirement is covered. §3–4: setup and architecture.\n47\t  §5–17: the systems. §18: the content. §19: build order. §20: risks. §21: testing. §22: tuning defaults. §23: done\n48\t  and hand-off. Appendices: data schemas, the network messages, pose codes, events.\n49\t- Numbers are starting values. The important ones are dev sliders (§17, §22). Tune with the sliders and the Play Lab;\n50\t  don't hand-edit numbers spread through the code.\n51\t- \"Engine change E#\" means modify your copy of the engine (§3.6) and log it in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`, because the About\n52\t  screen and the exit interview ask what you changed and why.\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## 1. The game on one page\n57\t\n58\t**Pitch.** The Tumbleweed Junction Fiascos have lost 99 games in a row, and their owner has just bet their field on\n59\tthe Fiasco Cup, the most over-the-top football tournament on Earth. It is 7-on-7 arcade football: turbo sprints,\n60\tbone-rattling Big Hits, flaming Hot Streaks, trick plays, mascots running onto the field, laterals across the field\n61\tand announcers losing their minds, all in pixel art drawn by code, with gibberish voices.\n62\t\n63\t**Core loop (write it as one sentence at the top of `src/00-boot.js`):** *Call a play from big cards, snap, then run,\n64\tpass, juke and hit your way down the field in a 5–10 second burst of chaos, cheer the replay, and do it again before\n65\tthe clock runs out.*\n66\t\n67\t| | |\n68\t|---|---|\n69\t| Genres | Arcade sports (American football), with beat-'em-up hit feel and a light story campaign |\n70\t| Perspective | A sideline **Broadcast** camera: the field runs left to right, seen from above the near sideline (a custom view, pitch 50°). A rotated **Kick Cam** behind the kicker for field goals and PATs. Replay angles: low field level, isometric, reverse. Optional Sky and Blimp views on V. |\n71\t| Players | 1–4 local (couch: pads and shared keyboard), or 1–4 online in AI Hub sessions (shared world) |\n72\t| Modes | Story (THE FIASCO CUP: prologue plus 5 chapters), Exhibition, Practice (the tutorial drills), Hall of Fiasco (records), Options, About/Credits, the attract mode, the Dev mode (from the pause menu) |\n73\t| Length | Story about 45 minutes. An exhibition about 12 minutes with 2:00 quarters. A hub round about 5–6 minutes (one half). |\n74\t| Tone | A 1990s arcade sports broadcast turned to 11: hype-man announcers, small-town underdog movie, trash talk, puns, harmless cartoon violence (PG) |\n75\t| Look | my-3D2dge's native style: HD Humanoid rigs drawn as pixel art, `res: 'ps1'` with `expand`, lit scenes, weather, particles |\n76\t\n77\t**What makes it the source (the pillars every feature serves):**\n78\t1. **The play call and the snap.** Football's rhythm: a strategic beat (cards), then an explosive physical burst.\n79\t2. **Big plays that feel huge.** Hit-stop, shake, slow motion, comic text, the crowd on its feet, the announcers\n80\t   screaming, a replay.\n81\t3. **Spectacle.** Stadiums, crowds, cheerleaders, mascots, marching band, pyrotechnics, weather, night lights.\n82\t4. **Underdogs and trash talk.** Cartoon characters with big personalities and a clear villain.\n83\t5. **Couch-to-online competition.** Head-to-head and co-op from 1 to 4 players.\n84\t\n85\t**Fiasco rules** (arcade simplifications, said on the loading tips and the Help screen): 7 per side; 4 downs to gain\n86\t**20 yards**; no penalties; late hits allowed (and celebrated); laterals any time; turbo for everyone.\n87\t\n88\t---\n89\t\n90\t## 2. TASK.md coverage map\n91\t\n92\t| TASK.md asks for | Where in this plan |\n93\t|---|---|\n94\t| Genre and perspective that best fit | §1, §5.6, §11.1 |\n95\t| Title from 50 brainstormed with a real RNG | §0.1 |\n96\t| Single HTML file; CDN deps allowed (none needed) | §3.5 |\n97\t| Built on the engine handoff, which may be changed | §3, §3.6 |\n98\t| Engine's native art style plus new assets for the source | §12 |\n99\t| Engine's native animation plus new animations | §12.3–12.5 |\n100\t| Audio all in code; Animalese/Simlish voices | §13 |\n101\t| Online through AI Hub, and the game on its own | §16 |\n102\t| Dev subfolder; bundle in the root; servers and processes etiquette | §0.2, §0.3, §3 |\n103\t| Performance unlimited (detail, effects, lights, shadows) | §11.5, §12.7 |\n104\t| Headless tests, scripted whole-game playthrough, one command | §21 |\n105\t| Feature-complete demo showing every mechanic; vertical-slice infrastructure | §15, §18, §19 |\n106\t| Dev mode from the pause menu; live sliders with reset to defaults | §17 |\n107\t| Attract mode after 10 s idle; AI may cheat; any input exits | §15.4 |\n108\t| Multiplayer through the hub protocol | §16 |\n109\t| Narrative tone like the source | §15.1, §18.6 |\n110\t| 10-foot, controller-first UI; native keyboard and mouse; device-following prompts; PS glyphs plus an Xbox option; no dense web screens | §9, §14 |\n111\t| Touch-only: \"Controller or keyboard required\", the Settings button, attract runs | §14.12 |\n112\t| No TODOs or stubs | §0.3, §23 |\n113\t| Source Information, feedback, About screen, exit-interview.json | §14.10, §23 |\n114\t\n115\tSTAGE.md's own list, for the plan:\n116\t\n117\t| STAGE.md asks the plan to cover | Where |\n118\t|---|---|\n119\t| The title first, picked as TASK.md says | the heading, §0.1 |\n120\t| Architecture and modules | §3, §4 |\n121\t| Every system and mechanic | §5–§17 |\n122\t| Levels | stadiums §18.2, story chapters §15.1, drills §15.2, attract segments §15.4 |\n123\t| Enemies | the opposing teams and their AI §18.1, §8; bosses and their specials §10.5 |\n124\t| Items | the Fiasco Cards and the Fiasco Meter §10.2; halftime adjustments §18.6 |\n125\t| Story beats | §15.1, §18.6 |\n126\t| Build order with milestones | §19 |\n127\t| The riskiest parts and how to de-risk them | §20 |\n128\t| How to test each part | §21, plus each milestone's exit check in §19 |\n129\t\n130\t---\n131\t\n132\t## 3. Tech stack and setup\n133\t\n134\t### 3.1 What you build on\n135\t- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2**, the handoff package (`dist/handoff/` of github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge,\n136\t  commit `4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72`, 2026-11-03). `my-3d2dge.js` there is the full engine with every\n137\t  module (1.2 MB, readable). Read `API.md` whole. Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names. From `AI_GUIDE.md`, read these\n138\t  sections: Visual craft, Animation, Hard rules, Standard features, Frame order, Systems in more depth (Views,\n139\t  Scenes, Camera, Players, Motion and timing, Body, Attack, Humanoid, Audio, Text and UI, Shell, Lighting, Testing\n140\t  without a browser, Recorded sessions), the Sports recipe, Building a big game, Where to find more, and the\n141\t  Checklist. Read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before writing shapes. Copy patterns from `kits/sports.js` (the\n142\t  SPORTS kit: the crowd on the stands, goalposts drawn with `r.queue`, the ball's shadow, `game.allowJoin`, the radio\n143\t  commentary through `E.Dialog`) and `game/test.mjs` (the headless test).\n144\t- **AI Hub client:** `lib/hub/session.js` **1.4.0** from github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub (commit\n145\t  `f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e`). Paste it unmodified. Read `lib/hub/README.md` whole, and\n146\t  `lib/hub/examples/arena.html` for the shared-world pattern this plan follows.\n147\t- **Motion clips:** the repository's `src/mocap/readable.js` and `src/mocap/mocap.js` (the runtime, 49 KB) and a set\n148\t  cut with `tools/anim-set.mjs` (§12.4). Clips are CC0 (Quaternius, Mesh2Motion) and free for any use (CMU). Credit\n149\t  them on the About screen.\n150\t- **No other runtime libraries.** Canvas 2D and Web Audio through the engine.\n151\t- **Tooling:** Node 24 (v24.22.1 here), Playwright **1.63.0** (pinned by the handoff's `package.json`; Chromium 1243 is\n152\t  already in `~/.cache/ms-playwright`), Python 3 for the local server.\n153\t\n154\t### 3.2 Fetching (sparse, into your dev folder)\n155\tThe full engine repository is large (its `examples/` alone is 79 MB) and the AI Hub repository is over 1 GB. Fetch\n156\tonly what you need:\n157\t```sh\n158\tcd AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831\n159\tmkdir -p .cache && cd .cache\n160\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git engine\n161\tgit -C engine sparse-checkout set dist/handoff src/mocap tools\n162\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub.git hub\n163\tgit -C hub sparse-checkout set lib/hub\n164\tgit -C engine log -1 --format=%H; git -C hub log -1 --format=%H   # record them in ENGINE-CHANGES.md\n165\t```\n166\tIf HEAD has moved past the commits above, check that `dist/handoff/README.md` still says v0.85.2 and `session.js`\n167\tsays 1.4.0. If they don't, fetch the pinned commits (`git fetch --depth 1 origin <sha>` then `git checkout <sha>`).\n168\t`.cache/` is never bundled and may be deleted at the end.\n169\t\n170\t### 3.3 The dev folder\n171\t```\n172\tAmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831/\n173\t  package.json         type: module; scripts: test, build, shots; devDependencies: playwright 1.63.0\n174\t  index.html           the dev page: loads vendor/ then src/ in order (open with file://)\n175\t  build.mjs            the bundler: writes ../<deliverable>.html and ../exit-interview.json\n176\t  ENGINE-CHANGES.md    every engine change: what, where, why (feeds the About screen)\n177\t  vendor/\n178\t    my-3d2dge.js       the engine (your modified copy of dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js)\n179\t    session.js         the AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte for byte\n180\t    mocap/readable.js  mocap/mocap.js   from the engine repository's src/mocap\n181\t    anim/fiasco-anim.js                 generated (§12.4)\n182\t    assets/fiasco-store.js              generated: the few store props used (§12.6)\n183\t  src/                 the game, one file per module, numbered in load order (§4.3)\n184\t  tools/               copied from the handoff: check.mjs filmstrip.mjs replay.mjs (standalone; they import playwright)\n185\t  test/                run.mjs (the one command), lib/, suites/, browser/, hub/ (harness.html bridge.js round.js)\n186\t  shots/               screenshots (output only)\n187\t```\n188\t**Run the asset and mocap tools in place, from `.cache/`.** They find the engine and the asset store relative to\n189\ttheir own folder, so copies in `tools/` would miss both:\n190\t- `node .cache/engine/dist/handoff/tools/asset-find.mjs \"<words>\"`\n191\t- `.../asset-set.mjs` (§12.6)\n192\t- `.../asset-sheet.mjs $PWD/src/24-shapes.js#<id>` (keep `24-shapes.js` free of `FF`: it may only use `My3D2dge`)\n193\t- `node .cache/engine/tools/anim-set.mjs` (§12.4)\n194\t`npm install` in the dev folder is enough for every tool: Node resolves `playwright` up the folder tree from\n195\t`.cache/` to the dev folder's `node_modules`.\n196\t\n197\t### 3.4 The dev page\n198\t`index.html` copies the handoff template's page: the `e3-boot` splash markup at the start of `<body>`, the canvas\n199\t`#screen`, then one `<script src>` per file. `session.js` goes first in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`\n200\t(§16.2). Next come the `hub:` meta tags, then in the body the engine, mocap/readable.js, mocap/mocap.js, the anim\n201\tset, the store props, and `src/*.js` in order. Opening it from `file://` must play the whole game.\n202\t\n203\t### 3.5 The bundle (`node build.mjs`)\n204\t1. Read `index.html`. Replace each `<script src=\"X\"></script>` with `<script>` + the file's text + `</script>`.\n205\t   Escape every `</script` inside a file's text as `<\\/script` (the engine and the anim set contain strings that need\n206\t   it). Keep `session.js` byte-identical apart from that wrapper, and keep it the first script in `<head>`.\n207\t2. Inline `src/about.json` as `window.FF_ABOUT = {...}`, and write `../exit-interview.json` from the same object\n208\t   (§23), so the About screen and the file can never disagree.\n209\t3. Write `../AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831.html`. Expect about 2 MB.\n210\t4. Print the size and the SHA-256 of the bundled `session.js` part, and check that it matches the vendor file.\n211\t\n212\tThe bundle needs no network. Don't use CDN dependencies: everything is already local.\n213\t\n214\t### 3.6 Engine changes to make (log each in ENGINE-CHANGES.md)\n215\tKeep the changes small and general. Each one is an engine feature a future game would want too.\n216\t\n217\t| # | Change | Where (in `my-3d2dge.js` v0.85.2) | Why |\n218\t|---|---|---|---|\n219\t| E1 | Add the glyphs `✕ ○ □ △` to the 5×7 font `G57` and the 3×5 `GLYPHS` | the PIXEL FONT section (search `const G57 =`) | `input.label()` returns `'✕'` and similar for a PlayStation pad, but the pixel font draws `?` for them |\n220\t| E2 | The `glyphs` setting defaults to `'playstation'`, its options are `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox']]`, and it shows on the Settings screen under a \"Controller\" heading as well as on the Controls screen | the standard settings table (search `glyphs: { group: 'Controller'`) and `Shell._settingsRows` | TASK.md: PlayStation glyphs, with a Settings option for Xbox |\n221\t| E3 | A **gear hook** on the HD Humanoid: `o.gear(api)` called in `_drawHD` just before `ops.sort(...)`, with `api = { g, view, Sp, S, Q, J, C, u, cap, limb, push(d, fn), rig }` | `_drawHD` (search `ops.sort((a, b) => a.d - b.d)`, the first one) | Facemasks, shoulder pads, jersey numbers, the carried ball, pom-poms and mascot heads, depth-sorted inside the rig |\n222\t| E4 | A `'football'` hat style in `Humanoid._hat`: a shell 1.12× the head, a center stripe (`h.stripe`), an ear logo dot (`h.logo`), and a grey facemask of 2 bars plus a vertical bar in front of the face | `_hat` (search `st === 'helmet'`) | A helmet that reads as football from every view |\n223\t| E5 | `E.Dialog`'s `voice` may be a function: `voice(ch, i, text, dialog)` is called for each newly typed letter instead of `audio.sfx(voice)` | `Dialog.update` (search `if (this.voice && Math.floor(this.shown) > prev`) | The Animalese voice synth (§13.4) |\n224\t| E6 | Audio buses: `audio.bus(name)` returns a GainNode under the master that follows mute and volume, and its volume follows a setting (`voices`, `crowd`) | the AUDIO section, after `_init` | Voices and the crowd bed need their own sliders and long-lived nodes |\n225\t| E7 | `Shell.show(name, { rows: [settingKeys] })`: any screen may list settings rows, and settings in a group whose name starts with `Dev` never show on the standard Settings screen | `Shell._render` (search `if (name === 'settings') this._settingsRows(add);`) and `_settingsRows` | The Dev mode's live sliders (§17) reuse the engine's slider rows |\n226\t| E8 | `game.menu('settings', { pause: false })` opens Settings without pausing | where `game.menu` is defined | In a hub session the world must not freeze when one player opens Settings (§16.6) |\n227\t| E9 | 10-foot Shell theme: an option `new E.Shell(game, { scale: 1.6 })` that scales the Shell's CSS (fonts, buttons, sliders, panel widths) by a factor, with `clamp()` by viewport | `SHELL_CSS` and the Shell constructor (or inject your own stylesheet with selectors stronger than `.esh`) | The standard pause, settings and controls screens are built for a desk at 14 px. A TV at 10 feet needs about 24 px or more. |\n228\t\n229\tAnything else you change, log it the same way. After the engine changes, `node tools/check.mjs` on the handoff's\n230\t`kits/sports.html` must still pass. Keep a copy of the untouched handoff in `.cache/` to diff against.\n231\t\n232\t---\n233\t\n234\t## 4. Architecture\n235\t\n236\t### 4.1 Principles\n237\t1. **The sim and the presentation are split.** The match simulation (`FF.sim`) owns athletes, the ball, rules and the\n238\t   clock in world units. It never reads a rig, a camera or the DOM. The presentation (rigs, camera, HUD, FX,\n239\t   commentary, audio) reads the sim's state and its events. This gives fast headless tests (presentation off), network\n240\t   clients that only render, and replays."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b4cfa638-847f-4819-b536-9be2b027efa5","uuid":"8b9da287-b886-4508-b3de-8d8ce1f5cb3a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:08:06.890Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261104-070752__xb697dc/work/PLAN.md","content":"# AMERICAN FOOTBALL FIASCO\n\n**The build plan.** Source: American football (over-the-top arcade style). Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the\nhandoff package). Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1. Planned on 2026-11-03 by claude-opus-5-5 (reasoning effort max)\nfrom `TASK.md` and `STAGE.md`. This plan is for the developers who will build the game from `TASK.md` and this file\nalone, possibly other AI models working without the planner. Every engine and hub name it uses was checked against\nthe real files during planning (engine commit `4390a9852fe0`, hub commit `f8835e4824fb`).\n\n---\n\n## 0. Read this first\n\n### 0.1 The title\nPicked the way TASK.md asks. 50 playful riffs on \"American Football\" were brainstormed silently and one was drawn\nwith a real random number generator: Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`, which uses OS entropy, gave index 33, which is\n**American Football Fiasco**. It shows on the title screen as `AMERICAN FOOTBALL FIASCO`. Do not re-roll it.\n\n### 0.2 Names and deliverables\n- GameTitle, file-safe: `AmericanFootballFiasco`. SOURCE, file-safe: `AmericanFootball`.\n- The dev folder (all dev files go here, inside this folder):\n  `AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831/`\n- The deliverable: `AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831.html` in this\n  folder's root, beside TASK.md. It is one self-contained HTML file and runs from `file://`.\n- `exit-interview.json` beside it, in exactly the shape TASK.md gives. Check that it parses.\n- If your TASK.md gives another model name or timestamp in those brackets, use your names and keep the GameTitle and\n  SOURCE parts.\n\n### 0.3 Working rules (from TASK.md, repeated here so none is missed)\n- Read only the files you need. Other agents share this directory, this machine's ports and its processes.\n- Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), run\n  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port it prints, and check that the page it serves is your game\n  before you trust a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID. Never use pkill, killall, `fuser -k`, or\n  kill whatever holds a port.\n- `/tmp` on this machine is a small tmpfs that other agents fill up (it was full during planning). Put clones, caches\n  and test output in your dev folder (`.cache/`, `shots/`, `check-output/`), not in `/tmp`.\n- Tests: one command that prints only failures and a one-line count (§21). Rerun it only after you change code it\n  covers. Take screenshots only for what a script can't check. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review,\n  judge or audit.\n- Done means no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features. Every feature in this plan ships, or this plan says how it\n  is cut.\n- The Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback go in the in-game About/Credits screen\n  and in your final chat reply. The same answers go in `exit-interview.json` (§23). Write \"unknown\" for anything you\n  can't confirm. The Plan field is `PLAN.md`.\n\n### 0.4 How to read this plan\n- §1–2: what the game is, and where each TASK.md requirement is covered. §3–4: setup and architecture.\n  §5–17: the systems. §18: the content. §19: build order. §20: risks. §21: testing. §22: tuning defaults. §23: done\n  and hand-off. Appendices: data schemas, the network messages, pose codes, events.\n- Numbers are starting values. The important ones are dev sliders (§17, §22). Tune with the sliders and the Play Lab;\n  don't hand-edit numbers spread through the code.\n- \"Engine change E#\" means modify your copy of the engine (§3.6) and log it in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`, because the About\n  screen and the exit interview ask what you changed and why.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game on one page\n\n**Pitch.** The Tumbleweed Junction Fiascos have lost 99 games in a row, and their owner has just bet their field on\nthe Fiasco Cup, the most over-the-top football tournament on Earth. It is 7-on-7 arcade football: turbo sprints,\nbone-rattling Big Hits, flaming Hot Streaks, trick plays, mascots running onto the field, laterals across the field\nand announcers losing their minds, all in pixel art drawn by code, with gibberish voices.\n\n**Core loop (write it as one sentence at the top of `src/00-boot.js`):** *Call a play from big cards, snap, then run,\npass, juke and hit your way down the field in a 5–10 second burst of chaos, cheer the replay, and do it again before\nthe clock runs out.*\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Genres | Arcade sports (American football), with beat-'em-up hit feel and a light story campaign |\n| Perspective | A sideline **Broadcast** camera: the field runs left to right, seen from above the near sideline (a custom view, pitch 50°). A rotated **Kick Cam** behind the kicker for field goals and PATs. Replay angles: low field level, isometric, reverse. Optional Sky and Blimp views on V. |\n| Players | 1–4 local (couch: pads and shared keyboard), or 1–4 online in AI Hub sessions (shared world) |\n| Modes | Story (THE FIASCO CUP: prologue plus 5 chapters), Exhibition, Practice (the tutorial drills), Hall of Fiasco (records), Options, About/Credits, the attract mode, the Dev mode (from the pause menu) |\n| Length | Story about 45 minutes. An exhibition about 12 minutes with 2:00 quarters. A hub round about 5–6 minutes (one half). |\n| Tone | A 1990s arcade sports broadcast turned to 11: hype-man announcers, small-town underdog movie, trash talk, puns, harmless cartoon violence (PG) |\n| Look | my-3D2dge's native style: HD Humanoid rigs drawn as pixel art, `res: 'ps1'` with `expand`, lit scenes, weather, particles |\n\n**What makes it the source (the pillars every feature serves):**\n1. **The play call and the snap.** Football's rhythm: a strategic beat (cards), then an explosive physical burst.\n2. **Big plays that feel huge.** Hit-stop, shake, slow motion, comic text, the crowd on its feet, the announcers\n   screaming, a replay.\n3. **Spectacle.** Stadiums, crowds, cheerleaders, mascots, marching band, pyrotechnics, weather, night lights.\n4. **Underdogs and trash talk.** Cartoon characters with big personalities and a clear villain.\n5. **Couch-to-online competition.** Head-to-head and co-op from 1 to 4 players.\n\n**Fiasco rules** (arcade simplifications, said on the loading tips and the Help screen): 7 per side; 4 downs to gain\n**20 yards**; no penalties; late hits allowed (and celebrated); laterals any time; turbo for everyone.\n\n---\n\n## 2. TASK.md coverage map\n\n| TASK.md asks for | Where in this plan |\n|---|---|\n| Genre and perspective that best fit | §1, §5.6, §11.1 |\n| Title from 50 brainstormed with a real RNG | §0.1 |\n| Single HTML file; CDN deps allowed (none needed) | §3.5 |\n| Built on the engine handoff, which may be changed | §3, §3.6 |\n| Engine's native art style plus new assets for the source | §12 |\n| Engine's native animation plus new animations | §12.3–12.5 |\n| Audio all in code; Animalese/Simlish voices | §13 |\n| Online through AI Hub, and the game on its own | §16 |\n| Dev subfolder; bundle in the root; servers and processes etiquette | §0.2, §0.3, §3 |\n| Performance unlimited (detail, effects, lights, shadows) | §11.5, §12.7 |\n| Headless tests, scripted whole-game playthrough, one command | §21 |\n| Feature-complete demo showing every mechanic; vertical-slice infrastructure | §15, §18, §19 |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu; live sliders with reset to defaults | §17 |\n| Attract mode after 10 s idle; AI may cheat; any input exits | §15.4 |\n| Multiplayer through the hub protocol | §16 |\n| Narrative tone like the source | §15.1, §18.6 |\n| 10-foot, controller-first UI; native keyboard and mouse; device-following prompts; PS glyphs plus an Xbox option; no dense web screens | §9, §14 |\n| Touch-only: \"Controller or keyboard required\", the Settings button, attract runs | §14.12 |\n| No TODOs or stubs | §0.3, §23 |\n| Source Information, feedback, About screen, exit-interview.json | §14.10, §23 |\n\nSTAGE.md's own list, for the plan:\n\n| STAGE.md asks the plan to cover | Where |\n|---|---|\n| The title first, picked as TASK.md says | the heading, §0.1 |\n| Architecture and modules | §3, §4 |\n| Every system and mechanic | §5–§17 |\n| Levels | stadiums §18.2, story chapters §15.1, drills §15.2, attract segments §15.4 |\n| Enemies | the opposing teams and their AI §18.1, §8; bosses and their specials §10.5 |\n| Items | the Fiasco Cards and the Fiasco Meter §10.2; halftime adjustments §18.6 |\n| Story beats | §15.1, §18.6 |\n| Build order with milestones | §19 |\n| The riskiest parts and how to de-risk them | §20 |\n| How to test each part | §21, plus each milestone's exit check in §19 |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tech stack and setup\n\n### 3.1 What you build on\n- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2**, the handoff package (`dist/handoff/` of github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge,\n  commit `4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72`, 2026-11-03). `my-3d2dge.js` there is the full engine with every\n  module (1.2 MB, readable). Read `API.md` whole. Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names. From `AI_GUIDE.md`, read these\n  sections: Visual craft, Animation, Hard rules, Standard features, Frame order, Systems in more depth (Views,\n  Scenes, Camera, Players, Motion and timing, Body, Attack, Humanoid, Audio, Text and UI, Shell, Lighting, Testing\n  without a browser, Recorded sessions), the Sports recipe, Building a big game, Where to find more, and the\n  Checklist. Read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before writing shapes. Copy patterns from `kits/sports.js` (the\n  SPORTS kit: the crowd on the stands, goalposts drawn with `r.queue`, the ball's shadow, `game.allowJoin`, the radio\n  commentary through `E.Dialog`) and `game/test.mjs` (the headless test).\n- **AI Hub client:** `lib/hub/session.js` **1.4.0** from github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub (commit\n  `f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e`). Paste it unmodified. Read `lib/hub/README.md` whole, and\n  `lib/hub/examples/arena.html` for the shared-world pattern this plan follows.\n- **Motion clips:** the repository's `src/mocap/readable.js` and `src/mocap/mocap.js` (the runtime, 49 KB) and a set\n  cut with `tools/anim-set.mjs` (§12.4). Clips are CC0 (Quaternius, Mesh2Motion) and free for any use (CMU). Credit\n  them on the About screen.\n- **No other runtime libraries.** Canvas 2D and Web Audio through the engine.\n- **Tooling:** Node 24 (v24.22.1 here), Playwright **1.63.0** (pinned by the handoff's `package.json`; Chromium 1243 is\n  already in `~/.cache/ms-playwright`), Python 3 for the local server.\n\n### 3.2 Fetching (sparse, into your dev folder)\nThe full engine repository is large (its `examples/` alone is 79 MB) and the AI Hub repository is over 1 GB. Fetch\nonly what you need:\n```sh\ncd AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831\nmkdir -p .cache && cd .cache\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git engine\ngit -C engine sparse-checkout set dist/handoff src/mocap tools\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub.git hub\ngit -C hub sparse-checkout set lib/hub\ngit -C engine log -1 --format=%H; git -C hub log -1 --format=%H   # record them in ENGINE-CHANGES.md\n```\nIf HEAD has moved past the commits above, check that `dist/handoff/README.md` still says v0.85.2 and `session.js`\nsays 1.4.0. If they don't, fetch the pinned commits (`git fetch --depth 1 origin <sha>` then `git checkout <sha>`).\n`.cache/` is never bundled and may be deleted at the end.\n\n### 3.3 The dev folder\n```\nAmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831/\n  package.json         type: module; scripts: test, build, shots; devDependencies: playwright 1.63.0\n  index.html           the dev page: loads vendor/ then src/ in order (open with file://)\n  build.mjs            the bundler: writes ../<deliverable>.html and ../exit-interview.json\n  ENGINE-CHANGES.md    every engine change: what, where, why (feeds the About screen)\n  vendor/\n    my-3d2dge.js       the engine (your modified copy of dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js)\n    session.js         the AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte for byte\n    mocap/readable.js  mocap/mocap.js   from the engine repository's src/mocap\n    anim/fiasco-anim.js                 generated (§12.4)\n    assets/fiasco-store.js              generated: the few store props used (§12.6)\n  src/                 the game, one file per module, numbered in load order (§4.3)\n  tools/               copied from the handoff: check.mjs filmstrip.mjs replay.mjs (standalone; they import playwright)\n  test/                run.mjs (the one command), lib/, suites/, browser/, hub/ (harness.html bridge.js round.js)\n  shots/               screenshots (output only)\n```\n**Run the asset and mocap tools in place, from `.cache/`.** They find the engine and the asset store relative to\ntheir own folder, so copies in `tools/` would miss both:\n- `node .cache/engine/dist/handoff/tools/asset-find.mjs \"<words>\"`\n- `.../asset-set.mjs` (§12.6)\n- `.../asset-sheet.mjs $PWD/src/24-shapes.js#<id>` (keep `24-shapes.js` free of `FF`: it may only use `My3D2dge`)\n- `node .cache/engine/tools/anim-set.mjs` (§12.4)\n`npm install` in the dev folder is enough for every tool: Node resolves `playwright` up the folder tree from\n`.cache/` to the dev folder's `node_modules`.\n\n### 3.4 The dev page\n`index.html` copies the handoff template's page: the `e3-boot` splash markup at the start of `<body>`, the canvas\n`#screen`, then one `<script src>` per file. `session.js` goes first in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`\n(§16.2). Next come the `hub:` meta tags, then in the body the engine, mocap/readable.js, mocap/mocap.js, the anim\nset, the store props, and `src/*.js` in order. Opening it from `file://` must play the whole game.\n\n### 3.5 The bundle (`node build.mjs`)\n1. Read `index.html`. Replace each `<script src=\"X\"></script>` with `<script>` + the file's text + `</script>`.\n   Escape every `</script` inside a file's text as `<\\/script` (the engine and the anim set contain strings that need\n   it). Keep `session.js` byte-identical apart from that wrapper, and keep it the first script in `<head>`.\n2. Inline `src/about.json` as `window.FF_ABOUT = {...}`, and write `../exit-interview.json` from the same object\n   (§23), so the About screen and the file can never disagree.\n3. Write `../AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831.html`. Expect about 2 MB.\n4. Print the size and the SHA-256 of the bundled `session.js` part, and check that it matches the vendor file.\n\nThe bundle needs no network. Don't use CDN dependencies: everything is already local.\n\n### 3.6 Engine changes to make (log each in ENGINE-CHANGES.md)\nKeep the changes small and general. Each one is an engine feature a future game would want too.\n\n| # | Change | Where (in `my-3d2dge.js` v0.85.2) | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| E1 | Add the glyphs `✕ ○ □ △` to the 5×7 font `G57` and the 3×5 `GLYPHS` | the PIXEL FONT section (search `const G57 =`) | `input.label()` returns `'✕'` and similar for a PlayStation pad, but the pixel font draws `?` for them |\n| E2 | The `glyphs` setting defaults to `'playstation'`, its options are `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox']]`, and it shows on the Settings screen under a \"Controller\" heading as well as on the Controls screen | the standard settings table (search `glyphs: { group: 'Controller'`) and `Shell._settingsRows` | TASK.md: PlayStation glyphs, with a Settings option for Xbox |\n| E3 | A **gear hook** on the HD Humanoid: `o.gear(api)` called in `_drawHD` just before `ops.sort(...)`, with `api = { g, view, Sp, S, Q, J, C, u, cap, limb, push(d, fn), rig }` | `_drawHD` (search `ops.sort((a, b) => a.d - b.d)`, the first one) | Facemasks, shoulder pads, jersey numbers, the carried ball, pom-poms and mascot heads, depth-sorted inside the rig |\n| E4 | A `'football'` hat style in `Humanoid._hat`: a shell 1.12× the head, a center stripe (`h.stripe`), an ear logo dot (`h.logo`), and a grey facemask of 2 bars plus a vertical bar in front of the face | `_hat` (search `st === 'helmet'`) | A helmet that reads as football from every view |\n| E5 | `E.Dialog`'s `voice` may be a function: `voice(ch, i, text, dialog)` is called for each newly typed letter instead of `audio.sfx(voice)` | `Dialog.update` (search `if (this.voice && Math.floor(this.shown) > prev`) | The Animalese voice synth (§13.4) |\n| E6 | Audio buses: `audio.bus(name)` returns a GainNode under the master that follows mute and volume, and its volume follows a setting (`voices`, `crowd`) | the AUDIO section, after `_init` | Voices and the crowd bed need their own sliders and long-lived nodes |\n| E7 | `Shell.show(name, { rows: [settingKeys] })`: any screen may list settings rows, and settings in a group whose name starts with `Dev` never show on the standard Settings screen | `Shell._render` (search `if (name === 'settings') this._settingsRows(add);`) and `_settingsRows` | The Dev mode's live sliders (§17) reuse the engine's slider rows |\n| E8 | `game.menu('settings', { pause: false })` opens Settings without pausing | where `game.menu` is defined | In a hub session the world must not freeze when one player opens Settings (§16.6) |\n| E9 | 10-foot Shell theme: an option `new E.Shell(game, { scale: 1.6 })` that scales the Shell's CSS (fonts, buttons, sliders, panel widths) by a factor, with `clamp()` by viewport | `SHELL_CSS` and the Shell constructor (or inject your own stylesheet with selectors stronger than `.esh`) | The standard pause, settings and controls screens are built for a desk at 14 px. A TV at 10 feet needs about 24 px or more. |\n\nAnything else you change, log it the same way. After the engine changes, `node tools/check.mjs` on the handoff's\n`kits/sports.html` must still pass. Keep a copy of the untouched handoff in `.cache/` to diff against.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Architecture\n\n### 4.1 Principles\n1. **The sim and the presentation are split.** The match simulation (`FF.sim`) owns athletes, the ball, rules and the\n   clock in world units. It never reads a rig, a camera or the DOM. The presentation (rigs, camera, HUD, FX,\n   commentary, audio) reads the sim's state and its events. This gives fast headless tests (presentation off), network\n   clients that only render, and replays.","numLines":240,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2515}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VKL5NnGzyqNVnA5xsjJsXk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
